@page "/proof"
@inject IWebAssemblyHostEnvironment HostEnv
@inject IOptions<AppConfig> Config

<PageTitle>Findings Proof — BlazorEnvDemo</PageTitle>

<h1>🔬 Detailed Findings Proof</h1>

<p>This page explains each finding from the review and shows live evidence using this running instance.</p>

<section>
    <h2>Finding: Environment defaults</h2>
    <table class="findings-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Scenario</th>
                <th>Expected Client Env</th>
                <th>This Instance</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>dotnet run</code> (no property set)</td>
                <td>Development</td>
                <td>@(HostEnv.IsDevelopment() ? "✅ Development" : "N/A")</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>dotnet publish</code> (no property set)</td>
                <td>Production</td>
                <td>@(HostEnv.IsProduction() ? "✅ Production" : "N/A")</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>dotnet publish -p:WasmApplicationEnvironmentName=UAT</code></td>
                <td>UAT</td>
                <td>@(HostEnv.IsEnvironment("UAT") ? "✅ UAT" : "N/A")</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">Only one row will show ✅ — that's the row matching how this instance was built/published.</p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Finding: Server ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT does NOT set client bundle env</h2>
    <div class="finding-box finding-important">
        <strong>Key insight:</strong> <code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=UAT</code> on Azure App Service
        or IIS controls only the <em>server-side</em> runtime (API, middleware, configuration).
        The client-side Blazor WASM bundle reads its environment from the
        <code>blazor.boot.json</code> artifact baked in at publish time.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Proof:</strong> This page's environment is <code>@HostEnv.Environment</code>.
        If this were changed solely by App Service's application setting, it would always
        change live without republishing — but it does not. Only republishing with a different
        <code>WasmApplicationEnvironmentName</code> changes it.
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Finding: launchSettings.json is ignored at publish</h2>
    <div class="finding-box finding-warning">
        <strong><code>Properties/launchSettings.json</code> sets the environment for
        <code>dotnet run</code> only.</strong> It is never read during
        <code>dotnet publish</code>. Relying on it to set UAT or Production environments
        for deployed artifacts will always fail silently — the client bundle will fall back to
        <code>Production</code>.
    </div>
    <p>
        See <code>Properties/launchSettings.json</code> in this project — it sets
        <code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Development</code> for the local dev server.
        That has no effect on any published output.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Finding: Pipeline-driven publish (zero repo commits)</h2>
    <p>The following publish command was used to produce this instance (or would be in its pipeline stage):</p>
    <pre><code>dotnet publish BlazorEnvDemo.csproj \
    -c Release \
    -p:WasmApplicationEnvironmentName=@HostEnv.Environment \
    -o ./publish/@HostEnv.Environment</code></pre>
    <p>
        No <code>.csproj</code> changes, no <code>appsettings</code> commits, no branch per environment needed.
        The stage variable drives everything.
    </p>
</section>

